<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mau movement</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mau was a non-violent movement for Samoan self-government. An early Mau a Pule under the orator chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lauaki-namulauulu-mamoe">Lauaki Namulauʻulu Mamoe</a> opposed German rule in 1908–1909, and the movement revived in the 1920s against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> rule in Western Samoa, where police killed its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupua-tamasese-lealofi-iii">Tupua Tamasese Lealofi III</a> and others on 'Black Saturday' in 1929. A smaller Mau in American Samoa protested US naval rule.</p></div>
